      question: "Describe and explain what happens to the particles in a substance as it is heated from a solid, through melting, to a gas. Include energy and temperature changes, and what happens at the melting and boiling points. (7 marks)\n\n```mermaid\nflowchart LR\n    A[SOLID\nParticles vibrate in\nfixed positions] -->|Heating — energy\nadded; temperature rises| B[MELTING POINT\nTemperature constant;\nenergy breaks bonds]\n    B -->|Melted —\nlatent heat of fusion| C[LIQUID\nParticles flow;\nbonds weakened]\n    C -->|Heating — energy\nadded; temperature rises| D[BOILING POINT\nTemperature constant;\nenergy breaks remaining bonds]\n    D -->|Boiling —\nlatent heat of vaporisation| E[GAS\nParticles free;\nhigh KE]\n```",
      answer: "In a solid: particles are in a regular lattice, vibrating about fixed positions. As energy is added by heating, particles vibrate more vigorously, so temperature rises. At the melting point: temperature stops rising even though energy is still being added. This energy (latent heat of fusion) is used to break the intermolecular bonds holding particles in fixed positions, not to increase kinetic energy. The particles can now move past each other — forming a liquid. As the liquid is heated, particle kinetic energy and temperature increase. At the boiling point: energy added (latent heat of vaporisation) is again used to completely overcome intermolecular attractions, not to raise temperature. Particles escape to form a gas — particles move quickly in random directions with much larger average separation. Gas temperature can then increase as kinetic energy increases. Key point: temperature represents average kinetic energy of particles. During state changes, energy changes potential energy (bonding), not kinetic energy.",
