      question: "Analyse the factors that affect the stopping distance of a car and evaluate the dangers of driving in poor conditions. (7 marks)",
      answer: "Stopping distance = thinking distance + braking distance. Thinking distance = speed × reaction time. Factors increasing thinking distance (increasing reaction time): tiredness, alcohol, drugs, mobile phone use, illness. These impair the driver's ability to react quickly. Braking distance factors: (1) Speed — at twice the speed, braking distance is four times greater (KE = ½mv², so KE increases with v², meaning braking must absorb much more energy). (2) Road conditions — wet, icy or greasy roads reduce friction between tyres and road, increasing braking distance. (3) Tyre condition — worn or underinflated tyres reduce grip. (4) Brake condition — worn brake pads reduce braking force. Dangers of poor conditions: ice can increase stopping distance by 10 times. A driver on a motorway at 70 mph (31 m/s) in normal conditions stops in ~96 m. On ice this could exceed 200 m — meaning drivers must increase following distance enormously. Conclusion: drivers should reduce speed in poor conditions, ensure vehicles are well maintained, and never drive impaired.",
