      question: "Describe the lifecycle of a star from nebula to final state. Your answer should cover stars similar to the Sun and more massive stars. (7 marks)\n\n```mermaid\nflowchart TD\n    A[Nebula — cloud of\ngas and dust] --> B[Protostar — gravity\npulls cloud together\ntemperature rises]\n    B --> C[Main Sequence Star\nnuclear fusion of hydrogen\nto helium — stable for\nbillions of years]\n    C --> D{Mass of star?}\n    D -->|Similar to our Sun\nsmaller mass| E[Red Giant — hydrogen\nin outer shell expands]\n    D -->|Much larger\nthan our Sun| F[Red Supergiant\nmassive expansion]\n    E --> G[Planetary Nebula\nouter layers shed]\n    G --> H[White Dwarf\ndense hot remnant\nno more fusion]\n    H --> I[Black Dwarf\ncooled white dwarf]\n    F --> J[Supernova\ncatastrophic explosion\nheavy elements formed]\n    J --> K{Core mass?}\n    K -->|Moderate| L[Neutron Star\nextremely dense]\n    K -->|Very large| M[Black Hole\ngravity so strong\nlight cannot escape]\n```",
      answer: "A star begins as a nebula — a cloud of hydrogen gas and dust held together by gravity. As it contracts under gravity the temperature rises, forming a protostar. When temperatures reach ~10 million °C, hydrogen nuclei fuse to form helium in nuclear fusion reactions, releasing enormous energy. The outward radiation pressure balances gravitational collapse — the star enters the stable main sequence phase. For a star like the Sun: when hydrogen in the core runs out, the core contracts and outer layers expand and cool → red giant. The outer layers are expelled as a planetary nebula leaving a white dwarf. It cools over billions of years to a black dwarf. For more massive stars: the red supergiant stage ends in a supernova — a massive explosion that creates and disperses heavy elements throughout the universe. The core remnant becomes either a neutron star or, if the core mass is very large, a black hole.",
